body:
Thanks for Anatomy. Audits of figures generated with the skill repeatedly found that enabling reduced motion during a gesture or fade did not stop it. Our generated code checked the preference before starting an action, but did not handle changes during it.

### Reproduce
1. Open an interactive figure with reduced motion off and start a gesture or fade.
2. While it is running, open Chrome DevTools → Rendering → Emulate CSS media feature `prefers-reduced-motion`, and select `reduce` without reloading.
3. The current animation continues. Expected: immediately settle the current action to its final state and remain still; controls should keep working.

### Relevant upstream code
At commit `1482e8469b0a9e0d8cc18ea2740a01423d29ae80`:
- [spot-plate/live.tsx, lines 132–164](https://github.com/wheresryan22/anatomy/blob/1482e8469b0a9e0d8cc18ea2740a01423d29ae80/examples/spot-plate/live.tsx#L132-L164): `aim()` checks `asksForStillness()` before starting, but the active `tick()` never rechecks or subscribes. A preference change therefore does not stop an in-flight interpolation.
- [Vanilla loop, lines 28–43](https://github.com/wheresryan22/anatomy/blob/1482e8469b0a9e0d8cc18ea2740a01423d29ae80/references/motion.md#L28-L43), followed by [dial-indicator, lines 224–239](https://github.com/wheresryan22/anatomy/blob/1482e8469b0a9e0d8cc18ea2740a01423d29ae80/examples/dial-indicator/build.mjs#L224-L239): these **do** read `.matches` each frame, but leave an already-running tour active; the dial spring also keeps integrating under `calm`. These patterns can carry the gap into generated figures.

This is not universal: [the React `useStillness()` hook already subscribes to `change`](https://github.com/wheresryan22/anatomy/blob/1482e8469b0a9e0d8cc18ea2740a01423d29ae80/kit/react/live.tsx#L11-L20).

### Suggested pattern
A `MediaQueryList` change listener fixed our in-flight cases. For example, in the dial's existing live script:

```js
function finishMotion() {
  cancelAnimationFrame(frame);
  frame = 0;
  clearTimeout(idleTimer);
  touring = false;
  x = target;
  tip.at = heightUnder(x);
  tip.rate = 0;
  draw();
}
const onMotionChange = () => {
  if (still.matches) finishMotion();
};
still.addEventListener("change", onMotionChange);
onMotionChange();
```

Other figures would finalize their own gesture/fade, clear pending motion timers and velocities, and restore final opacity. Remove the listener on teardown where applicable. Checking the preference at startup alone is insufficient.

A related generated-figure case: with `reduce` already active, explicitly selecting **continuous playback** should complete the remaining finite steps without animation. Returning early from the animation-loop starter otherwise leaves it doing nothing. This is distinct from the automatic idle tour, which should stay off. It would be useful to cover both cases in the authoring/check guidance.
